backup fail and unusable. Stream instead requires two replication connections and a client that keeps pace with WAL.\",\"status\":\"documented_fact\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html\",\"title\":\"pg_basebackup\",\"passage\":\"The write-ahead log files are collected at the end of the backup. Therefore, it is necessary for the source server's wal_keep_size parameter to be set high enough that the required log data is not removed before the end of the backup. If the required log data has been recycled before it's time to transfer it, the backup will fail and be unusable.\",\"locator\":\"--wal-method: fetch\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"},{\"url\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html\",\"title\":\"pg_basebackup\",\"passage\":\"Stream write-ahead log data while the backup is being taken. This method will open a second connection to the server and start streaming the write-ahead log in parallel while running the backup. Therefore, it will require two replication connections not just one. As long as the client can keep up with the write-ahead log data, using this method requires no extra write-ahead logs to be saved on the source server.\",\"locator\":\"--wal-method: stream\",\"origin\":\"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation\",\"sourceType\":\"Primary documentation\"}],\"method\":\"Applies specifically to pg_basebackup WAL collection modes. It is a documented precondition/failure mode, not measured operational reliability.\",\"comparability\":\"SQLite WAL-page content was fetched but no exact WAL passage could be extracted within the allowance; do not infer equivalence between SQLite WAL files and PostgreSQL archived/streamed WAL.\",\"confidence\":\"High for the stated pg_basebackup mode conditions because they are exact primary passages.\",\"disposition\":\"retain\"}],\"gaps\":[\"SQLite WAL documentation was fetched, but the retrieval allowance was exhausted before an exact WAL/checkpoint passage could be extracted. Highest-impact follow-up: retrieve the SQLite WAL documentation passage governing safe backup/copy conditions for an active WAL database.\",\"Neither documentation stream supplies comparable throughput, backup-window, restore-time, or fault-injection measurements; no performance or reliability ranking is supported.\",\"Current URLs were retrieved, but the extracted SQLite text and PostgreSQL body did not expose a publication date or version banner. The search result labeled PostgreSQL current documentation as version 18; this should be independently checked if a fixed-version conclusion is needed.\"],\"stopReason\":\"All five permitted retrieval invocations were used: one search, one four-URL primary-document fetch, and three bounded content retrievals. Both owned slots have direct primary support with the SQLite WAL-passage limitation explicitly named.\"}","usage":{"input":29760,"output":5603,"cacheRead":90112,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0.1447784},"exitCode":0,"stderr":"","finishedAt":1788761358680,"value":{"assignment":"backups","discovered":["extensions.web_search","extensions.fetch_content","extensions.get_search_content"],"calls":[{"ref":"extensions.web_search","args":{"queries":["site:sqlite.org backup API WAL online backup concurrent writes official documentation","site:postgresql.org docs current pg_basebackup online backup WAL recovery official documentation","site:postgresql.org docs current continuous archiving backup recovery pg_basebackup WAL required"],"numResults":5,"includeContent":false,"workflow":"none"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"3/3 queries successful; 15 total results.","responseId":"mtqu9m7ehjl7oo"},{"ref":"extensions.fetch_content","args":{"urls":["https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html","https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html"],"mode":"readable"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"4/4 official URLs fetched; 124513 total extracted characters.","responseId":"mtqu9r29s2o608"},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqu9r29s2o608","urlIndex":0,"findText":["The online backup API allows","The source database is read-locked","SQLite holds a write transaction open on the destination database"],"findMode":"case-insensitive"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"1 matching passage returned; 2 requested phrases had no match."},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqu9r29s2o608","urlIndex":2,"offset":0,"limit":30000},"retrieved":true,"detail":"Full 27980-character pg_basebackup document retrieved."},{"ref":"extensions.get_search_content","args":{"responseId":"mtqu9r29s2o608","urlIndex":3,"findText":["a continuous sequence of archived WAL files","starting with the file immediately following the one","base backup is not sufficient","restore_command","recovery.signal","test the recovery procedure"],"findMode":"case-insensitive"},"retrieved":true,"detail":"Matches returned for continuous WAL, restore_command, and recovery.signal; 3 requested phrases had no match."}],"rows":[{"id":"b1","slot":"backup","finding":"SQLite documents its Online Backup API as incremental copying in which the source is locked only during actual reads, rather than for the full copy.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html","title":"SQLite Backup API","passage":"The online backup API allows the contents of one database to be copied into another database file, replacing any original contents of the target database. The copy operation may be done incrementally, in which case the source database does not need to be locked for the duration of the copy, only for the brief periods of time when it is actually being read from.","locator":"Online Backup API introduction","origin":"SQLite Documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Current SQLite documentation URL retrieved. This supports API-level online-copy locking behavior, not a measured backup duration, throughput, or end-to-end restore reliability.","comparability":"Not directly comparable to PostgreSQL physical-backup requirements or performance; no matched workload measurement was retrieved.","confidence":"High for the stated API locking behavior because it is an exact primary-documentation passage.","disposition":"retain"},{"id":"b2","slot":"backup","finding":"PostgreSQL documents pg_basebackup as an online physical cluster backup; a standalone result requires WAL inclusion, while continuous-archive recovery requires uninterrupted archived WAL reaching back to the backup start.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"pg_basebackup is used to take a base backup of a running PostgreSQL database cluster. The backup is taken without affecting other clients of the database, and can be used both for point-in-time recovery","locator":"Description","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"Includes the required WAL (write-ahead log) files in the backup. This will include all write-ahead logs generated during the backup. Unless the method none is specified, it is possible to start a postmaster in the target directory without the need to consult the WAL archive, thus making the output a completely standalone backup.","locator":"--wal-method","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html","title":"25.3. Continuous Archiving and Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR)","passage":"To recover successfully using continuous archiving (also called “online backup” by many database